The condition to be guaranteed is the presence of at least one of the two specific pairs (round apple + star peach, or round peach + star apple). Equivalently, the complementary (avoiding) collections are those that contain neither pair.

The four families of avoiding collections, together with the maximum size of each, are obtained by enumerating the admissible combinations of the four apple/peach types:

- both apple shapes and no peaches: \(7+7+8+4=26\);
- both peach shapes and no apples: \(9+6+8+4=27\);
- both round apple/peach types (and therefore no star apple/peach): \(7+9+8+4=28\);
- both star apple/peach types (and therefore no round apple/peach): \(7+6+8+4=25\).

No larger avoiding collection exists: any three of the four apple/peach types already realises one of the two forbidden pairs, and watermelons may always be added in full without creating a forbidden pair. Consequently the largest number of sweets that can still fail the condition is 28. Drawing one more sweet forces the missing star-apple or star-peach piece and realises a required pair.

Thus 29 sweets suffice in every case, and 29 is the exact minimum that guarantees the condition.

Final answer: \(29\)

The goal is to find the smallest number \(n\) such that any selection of \(n\) candies is guaranteed to contain at least one of the two mixed-shape pairs (round apple together with pentagram peach, or round peach together with pentagram apple). Equivalently, \(n\) is one more than the size of a largest selection that avoids both mixed-shape pairs.

Any avoiding selection must omit at least one candy from each mixed-shape pair. The four maximal families of avoiding selections are therefore:

- all candies except the two pentagram apple/peach types (size \(7+9+8+4=28\));
- all candies except the two apple types (size \(9+6+8+4=27\));
- all candies except the two peach types (size \(7+7+8+4=26\));
- all candies except the two round apple/peach types (size \(7+6+8+4=25\)).

No larger avoiding selection exists, because adjoining even one extra candy to any of the four families immediately completes a mixed-shape pair. Consequently the largest avoiding selection has size 28, and 29 candies suffice to guarantee a mixed-shape apple-peach pair.

Final answer: \(29\)
